WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:08.730
SIERRA: You know you’ve made it to the highest
level of prestige when you get to meet the

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:09.730
Pope.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:16.030
Actors, artists, athletes and politicians
- they all clamor for a handshake with his

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:17.199
holiness.

00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:21.810
But aside from his starpower, the Pope also
has a lot of actual power.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:29.359
He runs a massive organization that serves
over 1.3 billion Catholics worldwide, nearly

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:36.040
a fifth of the global population.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.860
On any given issue, you may find the church’s
position agreeable or egregious.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:46.890
What you may not realize though is that for
some 2,000 years the Pope has been at the

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.039
center of global affairs.

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:54.199
He has a seat at the table alongside heads
of state, and is sought after for his distinct

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:56.360
form of influence and diplomacy.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.110
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why it
Matters.

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:04.510
Today, The Pope’s one-of-a-kind role in
international relations.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:15.619
Reagan Library: If men and women hope to transform
society, they must begin by changing their

00:01:15.619 --> 00:01:16.770
own hearts first.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:24.939
American Rhetoric Online Speech Bank: As a
nation faces increasingly complex political

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:33.210
and ethical issues of our time, I am confident
that the American people will find their religious

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:40.439
beliefs as a precious source of insight, to
live a more human and free society.

00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:48.640
CBS New York: Peace is not simply the absence
of war, but the work of justice.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.470
SIERRA: Let's dive right into it.

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:57.110
When you look at lists of the most powerful
people in the world that come out every year,

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.840
you often find the Pope in, you know, top
10.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:07.909
And he's next to people like Joe Biden or
Vladimir Putin or, you know, probably a Kardashian.

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:13.860
And, it occurs to me that I don't really understand
why the Pope is powerful.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.970
So is the Pope powerful, and what kind of
power does he wield?

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.840
BYRNES: The question of whether he's powerful
is a difficult question.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:21.840
I think he is.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.390
I think there are three reasons.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:27.690
First is, as we know, he leads this huge global
institution of the Catholic Church.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.599
There's over a billion Catholics.

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:35.100
He has ready access to them through encyclicals,
through sermons that priests and bishops give.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.580
So because of that and because I think he's
sort of prominently dressed and presented

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:45.060
as a religious figure in a world that takes
that seriously, he's surrounded by a media

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.330
circus of incredible intensity and attention.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:50.940
SIERRA: This is Timothy Byrnes.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.909
He is a professor of political science at
Colgate University, he’s also the author

00:02:55.909 --> 00:03:01.900
of four books about the Catholic church’s
role in politics and global affairs.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:10.549
BYRNES: And, since the middle of the 20th
century, popes have used modern forms of communication

00:03:10.549 --> 00:03:12.799
and travel to take advantage of this.

00:03:12.799 --> 00:03:16.209
So he's on Twitter, for example, Pontifex,
millions of followers.

00:03:16.209 --> 00:03:17.519
He travels all over the world.

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:21.980
So he's this major sort of religious figure
leading the largest single religious institution

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:22.980
in the world.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:23.980
That's one reason.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.180
But he's also the leader of the Catholic hierarchy
throughout the world.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:32.431
And by that, I mean every bishop in the world
is appointed by the Pope personally, and he

00:03:32.431 --> 00:03:35.670
is the leader of each national hierarchy or
conference.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:40.450
So when you think about world politics or
international relations, whatever country

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.630
is interacting with another country.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:46.950
There are Catholics in those countries, and
they are led by bishops who are appointed

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.950
by and led by the Pope.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.810
He's involved with all of these national hierarchies
all over the world.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:58.030
And then finally, and really kind of oddly,
to be honest with you, he's a formal participant

00:03:58.030 --> 00:03:59.970
in diplomatic relations.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:05.829
The Pope, the Holy See, at the moment has
diplomatic relations with 183 countries, including

00:04:05.829 --> 00:04:07.600
the United States, for example.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:13.030
So he has access to international relations
in a way that no other religious leader could

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:14.680
even imagine having.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.140
He's a permanent observer at the United Nations.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:22.220
He's invited as a formal participant in UN
conferences and conventions.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:29.669
SIERRA: Did you get that, relations with 183
countries, for perspective that’s more than

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:32.850
the U.S. and US does a lot of diplomacy.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:38.699
And this gets at the crux of it, the Pope’s
role on the world stage is not just symbolic,

00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:39.699
or religious.

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:44.449
He’s involved in negotiations and diplomacy
on a daily basis.

00:04:44.449 --> 00:04:50.720
SIERRA: Can you tell me a little bit about
the structure of the Vatican's foreign policy

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:51.720
arm?

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:52.720
Do they have a state department?

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:53.720
BYRNES: They do.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:57.830
There's a person who's called the Vatican
secretary of state, interestingly enough.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.920
There's two people below him.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:07.789
One runs the kind of internal church matters
and the other, the undersecretary for relations

00:05:07.789 --> 00:05:11.940
with states, he runs, they're all he, of course,
runs relations with states.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:16.220
So yes, it's a kind of a state department,
and they have country desks.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.800
They have regional leaders, just like any
state department would in a country.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.210
And then they have these ambassadors, they're
called nuncios or apostolic delegates, but

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.169
mostly nuncios.

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:33.130
Those are sent to these countries with which
the Vatican has diplomatic relations.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:38.660
And then interestingly enough, the countries
with whom the Holy See has diplomatic relations,

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.320
there are ambassadors from those countries
in Rome.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:49.699
So like the United States has an ambassador
who lives in Rome and who interacts with the

00:05:49.699 --> 00:05:56.819
Holy See and the Pope pretty much as any other
diplomat would.

00:05:56.819 --> 00:05:59.360
So he has this really unique place in the
world.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.669
He leads this huge religious institution,
which brings with it enormous amounts of,

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:08.449
I'm interested you mentioned, Kardashians,
a sort of celebrity focus of this global religious

00:06:08.449 --> 00:06:09.569
figure.

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:15.009
He heads the hierarchies in all countries
around the world as they interact with politics

00:06:15.009 --> 00:06:16.200
and international relations.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:22.810
And then he's a diplomatic legal player in
international relations and UN processes.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:28.090
SIERRA: Is the Pope the only religious leader
welcomed into these discussions?

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:34.490
BYRNES: Formally, yes, I mean you don't have
diplomatic relations with Islam or Orthodoxy

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.510
or Judaism or any other religion.

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:39.810
So in formal terms, for sure, he is.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:44.340
But there are other religious leaders, we
can think about Islamic leaders in Iran, for

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.590
example, who are certainly players in international
relations largely through their leadership

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:54.810
of an individual state, and so those religious
leaders do have a certain political role to

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:55.810
play.

00:06:55.810 --> 00:07:00.139
But in terms of transnational global religious
leaders, no, I'm waiting for someone to come

00:07:00.139 --> 00:07:03.760
along who can mention anyone who's even in
the same category.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.110
SIERRA: Before we go any further, there are
three phrases we’re using in this episode

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:11.520
- the pope, the Holy See, and the Vatican.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.370
They refer to different things, but they often
get used interchangeably.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:21.669
You can think about it sort of like the President,
the Executive Branch, and the White House.

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:25.449
One is a person, one is an organization, and
one is a place.

00:07:25.449 --> 00:07:31.430
The pope is the person in charge, the Holy
See is the organization he’s in charge of,

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:36.900
and the Vatican, or Vatican City - is the
place where that organization is located.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:44.050
BYRNES: The Vatican is the headquarters of
the international Catholic Church in Rome.

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:45.699
So it comprises St. Peter's.

00:07:45.699 --> 00:07:50.340
It comprises a bunch of other buildings that
are used administratively, residentially.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:57.120
Vatican City is about a 100-acre micro-state,
which was ceded to the church and the Holy

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.000
See in the 1920s as part of the Lateran Accord.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.090
So Vatican City if anyone's ever been there
as a tourist, that's when you walk into St.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:06.110
Peter's Square and you're no longer in Italy.

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:10.389
The Vatican are those buildings that house
the international headquarters of the Catholic

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:11.389
Church.

00:08:11.389 --> 00:08:13.050
SIERRA: So it's its own country to itself?

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.050
BYRNES: It is.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:17.530
SIERRA: Because I went as a kid, I don't remember
doing a passport thing.

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:21.819
BYRNES: No, there are no passports, but I'm
surprised you didn't do what my kids did,

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:26.370
which is they put one foot in Italy and one
foot in Vatican City and go back and forth.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:28.930
SIERRA: I mean that's pretty cool.

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:30.340
And the pope lives there?

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:31.340
BYRNES: Yeah.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:34.099
The Pope is the bishop of Rome, actually.

00:08:34.099 --> 00:08:38.560
He's elected by the College of Cardinals to
serve as the bishop of Rome and, as bishop

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:43.349
of Rome, he also serves as what's called the
Supreme Pontiff of the Catholic Church.

00:08:43.349 --> 00:08:48.460
So he is, in a sense, the head bishop of all
Catholics throughout the world.

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:53.170
SIERRA: And the president or king of the Vatican,
in that sense, then?

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:58.110
BYRNES: Well, again, I would say Vatican City,
he is the absolute ruler.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:00.570
They don't use the word, king, or certainly
not president.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:05.649
But he is, in fact, legally the absolute ruler
of the micro-state of Vatican City.

00:09:05.649 --> 00:09:12.040
SIERRA: So the pope has a unique combination
of institutional, diplomatic, and soft power.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:14.040
But what does this look like in reality?

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:18.850
Well, a lot of it is happening every day behind
the scenes.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:23.470
But every once in a while, these interactions
make history.

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:30.990
BYRNES: When Julian Assange released WikiLeaks,
one of the things that was released that drew

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:36.310
my attention, but maybe not yours, was that
there were a huge number of documents within

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:42.660
the United States State Department which reflected
the communications between the US State Department

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:44.390
and the Holy See.

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:45.760
And they were fascinating.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.470
This was under President George W. Bush and
Obama, mostly.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:55.399
The US diplomatic leaders acknowledged and
recognized that the Holy See was a unique

00:09:55.399 --> 00:10:00.089
actor in international relations because it
wasn't just a state.

00:10:00.089 --> 00:10:05.079
So you read cables and documents from State
Department leaders in the Obama administration,

00:10:05.079 --> 00:10:08.650
for example, saying like I would say, there's
nobody else like this.

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:15.089
These people are kind of diplomatic representatives
of a global institution, and we can work with

00:10:15.089 --> 00:10:20.399
them on all kinds of issues having to do with
Middle Eastern politics, having to do with

00:10:20.399 --> 00:10:25.529
climate change, having to do with diplomatic
relations with Cuba, which is a fascinating

00:10:25.529 --> 00:10:26.550
story I'd like to tell.

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:28.589
Sierra: I’d love to hear that story.

00:10:28.589 --> 00:10:33.269
BYRNES: So as I'm sure you know, the United
States broke off diplomatic relations with

00:10:33.269 --> 00:10:36.180
Cuba after the Castro revolution.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:39.730
The United States imposed an economic embargo
on Cuba for decades.

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:44.329
And, so the United States and Cuba not only
didn't have diplomatic relations, they almost

00:10:44.329 --> 00:10:45.720
didn't talk to each other at all.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:50.459
It was like a basic fact of American electoral
college dynamics that you just couldn't even

00:10:50.459 --> 00:10:52.850
talk about relations with Cuba.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:55.279
Obama was interested in reaching out to Cuba.

00:10:55.279 --> 00:11:00.040
This was just something he thought would be
a historic step that he could take, but he

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:01.601
knew that he couldn't do it himself.

00:11:01.601 --> 00:11:08.459
And so what he did was he contacted prominent
Catholic bishops in the US who, remember,

00:11:08.459 --> 00:11:12.670
are not only American citizens, they're also
bishops of the Catholic Church who are in

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:17.839
communion with the bishop of Rome, the Pope,
and have these kinds of relationships with

00:11:17.839 --> 00:11:19.339
other bishops around the world.

00:11:19.339 --> 00:11:25.329
And, so Catholic bishops in the US and Catholic
bishops in Cuba began to communicate to each

00:11:25.329 --> 00:11:31.840
other through diplomatic pouches sent from
the Holy See in the Vatican to the US State

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:34.040
Department in the United States.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:39.850
So the Vatican or the Holy See served as a
kind of a kind of honest broker with full

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:45.540
secrecy and discretion between the Cuban government
and the United States government as mediated

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:48.720
by Cuban bishops and American bishops.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:51.899
And then one day Barack Obama called a press
conference and said…

00:11:51.899 --> 00:11:53.160
New York Times, President Obama: Good afternoon.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:59.690
Today the United States of America is changing
its relationship with the people of Cuba,

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:05.420
in the most significant changes in our policy
in more than 50 years, we will end an outdated

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:12.260
approach that for decades has failed to advance
our interests, and instead we will begin to

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:15.410
normalize relations between our two countries.

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:19.519
BYRNES: And that was done through the church.

00:12:19.519 --> 00:12:25.850
SIERRA: The Vatican had contacts on both sides
- in part because there were large catholic

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:27.900
communities on both sides.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:31.920
And they used that trust to open a door that
had been closed for decades.

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:37.529
Now, we should mention that the Trump Administration
undid this diplomatic progress with Cuba a

00:12:37.529 --> 00:12:44.329
few years later, but that’s another story.

00:12:44.329 --> 00:12:47.660
The church is involved in negotiations all
the time.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:52.079
And to understand that better we wanted to
speak to someone who has been in the room

00:12:52.079 --> 00:12:53.959
during these discussions.

00:12:53.959 --> 00:12:59.620
For that we turn to Francis Campbell, formerly
a British Ambassador to the Holy See, he is

00:12:59.620 --> 00:13:03.680
now the president of the University of Notre
Dame in Australia.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:08.720
He was interviewed by Why It Matters producer
Asher Ross, who’s making his debut on the

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:09.720
show.

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:11.290
SIERRA: Hey Asher!

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:13.220
ROSS: Hey Gabby!

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:16.430
SIERRA: Okay take it away.

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:20.709
ROSS: On the international stage, we're used
to the idea that countries are supposed to

00:13:20.709 --> 00:13:23.450
act in their own interests, or the interest
of their citizens.

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:24.750
That's their essential goal.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:26.769
What is the Vatican's goal?

00:13:26.769 --> 00:13:27.920
What drives them?

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:31.540
CAMPBELL: One of the most obvious interests
it would pursue in different parts of the

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:33.339
world is religious freedom.

00:13:33.339 --> 00:13:36.699
Very broadly, it's pursuing human rights.

00:13:36.699 --> 00:13:40.180
It pursues aspects of disarmament, global
disarmament.

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:45.079
It pursues aspects very much around the eradication
of poverty.

00:13:45.079 --> 00:13:48.980
Very much around a whole social justice agenda.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:55.420
So it's there as a not directly wanting to
interfere in the internal workings of another

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:56.420
state.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:14:00.790
It would leave that to the local Catholic
church, but it operates in unison.

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:06.670
So at the U.N. level, you will find them very,
very much in favor of efforts towards disarmament,

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:09.800
efforts, towards international rapprochement.

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:12.790
A constant advocate for peace.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:20.300
So if one recalls the war in Iraq in 2003,
Pope John Paul II was really one of the world's

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:24.570
most vocal opponents of the war in Iraq.

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:26.970
And that would be a pretty consistent theme.

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:32.709
You would've seen the papacy intervene at
different stages of international crises.

00:14:32.709 --> 00:14:36.890
For example, Pope John the 23rd around the
time of the Cuban missile crisis.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:44.170
So they will lend the office of the papacy
as a potential sort of neutral broker if that

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:45.360
were ever required.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:49.509
ROSS: What I'm wondering is that the church
must have a separate set of goals that are

00:14:49.509 --> 00:14:51.240
theological or religious in nature.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:52.330
Their religious duties.

00:14:52.330 --> 00:14:57.540
And I'm wondering what intersection you see
between those religious duties and goals,

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:01.339
or missions, and these IR missions that you're
speaking about.

00:15:01.339 --> 00:15:07.389
CAMPBELL: Well, that's where it gets to a
really healthy tension for the papacy.

00:15:07.389 --> 00:15:12.769
And there's a real tension between what I
would describe the diplomat and the prophetic

00:15:12.769 --> 00:15:14.480
voice.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:19.269
For example, when the Vatican was dealing
in the Cold War with the countries of central

00:15:19.269 --> 00:15:24.800
Eastern Europe, you had a real debate between
the prophetic voice of the Vatican that wanted

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:29.589
to call this out what was happening with regards
to human rights, and religious freedom, and

00:15:29.589 --> 00:15:32.160
the totalitarian nature of the state.

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:38.300
And then the more diplomatic pragmatists that
were saying, we need some sort of pragmatic

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:42.529
deal in order to allow the church to function
in these countries.

00:15:42.529 --> 00:15:48.300
SIERRA: By the way, a lot of popes are mentioned
in this episode, and it’s understandable

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:50.970
if you start to get their names mixed up.

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:54.209
But there are two you’ll really want to
listen for.

00:15:54.209 --> 00:15:59.550
The first is the current one, Pope Francis,
who was elected in 2013.

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:04.399
So far, he's best known for advocating for
refugees and climate action.

00:16:04.399 --> 00:16:07.700
The other Pope to keep in mind is John Paul
II.

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:11.940
He led the Church from 1978 until his death
in 2005.

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:16.980
He served for a very long time, and enjoyed
a lot of popularity.

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:22.720
Among other things, he was pope during the
end of the Cold War and the fall of the USSR.

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:29.361
ROSS: Throughout our research on this show,
I keep hearing about John Paul II, the pope's

00:16:29.361 --> 00:16:33.310
role in Poland during the Cold War, and the
solidarity movement.

00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:37.540
And I was hoping that you could relate that
story to us, and kind of maybe give us your

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:41.320
thoughts about what that says about the pope's
influence in global affairs.

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:46.050
CAMPBELL: You know, this was the first time
that a pope was elected from Central and Eastern

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:47.050
Europe.

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:50.740
The first time in four-and-a-half centuries,
where there was a non-Italian elected.

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:56.610
And all of a sudden in 1978, you have the
Archbishop of Kraków elected pope at the

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:58.060
age of 58.

00:16:58.060 --> 00:17:04.220
So he's young, and he's coming from the church
behind the Iron Curtain.

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:09.809
So this is someone that immediately in the
Western world is able to give a different

00:17:09.809 --> 00:17:11.670
critique of communism.

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:14.780
They're able to give a lived critique of communism.

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:21.809
And with the rise of solidarity out of Gdańsk,
you see during John Paul's visits to Poland,

00:17:21.809 --> 00:17:29.840
and the first visit in 1979, and his speech
in Victory Square in Warsaw, you see the full

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:36.630
convening power of the papacy, and the full
voice, speaking to the people in a way that

00:17:36.630 --> 00:17:40.750
communism could never get hold of that level
of attention.

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:43.960
ROSS: What do you mean when you say convening
power?

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:48.179
CAMPBELL: Well, you see millions of people
coming to see him.

00:17:48.179 --> 00:17:51.870
And if you look in relation to Central and
Eastern Europe, and you look at the role of

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:56.679
John Paul II, at that particular point in
time, you are seeing someone that is holding

00:17:56.679 --> 00:18:04.059
up a higher standard and set of values, but
has an understanding of how communism is translating

00:18:04.059 --> 00:18:06.309
to the ordinary Pole.

00:18:06.309 --> 00:18:10.910
And how it is imposed rather than embraced.

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:15.990
So when you see him arriving in 1979, and
you see him traveling through Poland.

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:21.700
The communist authorities can't keep him out,
but people are naturally wanting to come to

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:25.549
listen to him, and it's not through coercion
or compulsion.

00:18:25.549 --> 00:18:27.500
It's a voluntary act.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:33.530
Whereas, at that period in time, Poland was
living under the imposition of communism.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:34.789
It wasn't embraced.

00:18:34.789 --> 00:18:40.980
So the contrast, the immediate direct contrast
of the images of millions of people coming

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:47.410
to listen to a spiritual leader, talking about
integral human development.

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:52.049
Talking about values, and the virtues that
go with the human spirit.

00:18:52.049 --> 00:18:57.330
You have a natural voice that is speaking
to Poland, in a way that Polish communists

00:18:57.330 --> 00:19:00.970
could not speak to Poland, and to the ordinary
citizenry.

00:19:00.970 --> 00:19:06.960
And that immediately set up a really different,
and difficult dynamic for the Soviet Union,

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:09.049
and it's then allies in the Warsaw pact.

00:19:09.049 --> 00:19:14.450
ROSS: So the people coming out into the streets
to hear him speak, they knew that this was

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:19.570
both, you know, Poland is a Catholic country
at this point, under Soviet rule.

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.330
And these people know, A) this is my spiritual
leader.

00:19:22.330 --> 00:19:25.590
But, B) this guy has been through what I've
been through.

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:27.320
He understands my experiences.

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:28.450
That's a potent mix.

00:19:28.450 --> 00:19:34.010
I mean what role do people believe that he
played in the subsequent end to the Cold War?

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:42.919
CAMPBELL: As regards his role in ending the
Cold War, he is certainly one of the key main

00:19:42.919 --> 00:19:43.919
figures.

00:19:43.919 --> 00:19:47.730
You know, he would share that with Gorbachev.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:50.230
He would share that with Reagan.

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:54.390
He would be one of those three or four people.

00:19:54.390 --> 00:19:58.490
And if you believe that Poland was the first
domino to go.

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:04.360
And if you believe then in the domino effect,
then there is no way that one couldn't say

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:11.580
that John Paul II was one of the three pivotal
figures in the demise of communism in Poland,

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:15.750
and indeed the demise of communism across
Central and Eastern Europe.

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:22.429
BYRNES: Pope John Paul II, basically articulated
in every way available to him that Soviet

00:20:22.429 --> 00:20:28.890
communist rule of Poland and the rest of Central
Europe was illegitimate and not permanent.

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:34.159
And of all people, Mikhail Gorbachev once
called Pope John Paul II the first citizen

00:20:34.159 --> 00:20:35.799
of global civil society.

00:20:35.799 --> 00:20:42.429
And, basically, what he did was he stood up
in Rome, and especially in his visits to Poland,

00:20:42.429 --> 00:20:45.450
Warsaw and Krakow, and waved his arms and
said, "We’re Poles.

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:48.080
We're Catholics.

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:54.830
We are not communists in some kind of transnational
Soviet way, and we need to be readmitted or

00:20:54.830 --> 00:21:00.909
rejoin a unified Europe that will allow us
to live," what he called, "an authentically

00:21:00.909 --> 00:21:02.750
Polish and Catholic life."

00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:07.640
SIERRA: John Paul’s long tenure, and his
activism, have ensured his place in the history

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:08.640
books.

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:09.971
Now, we have a new pope.

00:21:09.971 --> 00:21:11.630
Pope Francis.

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:17.950
CNBC: Pope Francis delivered a stern warning
to the leader of the Russian orthodox church.

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:21.700
His message, don’t be Vladimir Putin’s
alter boy.

00:21:21.700 --> 00:21:26.289
WION: The head of the Catholic Church and
sovereign of the Vatican City State has said

00:21:26.289 --> 00:21:31.760
the earth will become uninhabitable if there
is no contrary action taken on climate change.

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:37.140
BBC News: The pope came here with a profound
message on the treatment of refugees.

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:39.799
In essence he said, recognize their humanity.

00:21:39.799 --> 00:21:45.179
SIERRA: Pope Francis has a very different
style - I mean, heck he tweets! - and he’s

00:21:45.179 --> 00:21:49.539
speaking out about a different set of challenges,
like climate change, and the Russian invasion

00:21:49.539 --> 00:21:50.919
of Ukraine.

00:21:50.919 --> 00:21:56.350
He also serves during a time of ongoing reckoning
for the church, in which the public is sharply

00:21:56.350 --> 00:22:01.610
aware of atrocities committed by Catholic
priests, such as widespread sexual abuse or

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:05.779
crimes against indigenous children in residential
schools.

00:22:05.779 --> 00:22:11.450
Pope Francis has taken new steps in acknowledging
and apologizing for these crimes, though many

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:16.980
still say the church has not done nearly enough
to repair the damage.

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:23.549
SIERRA: Alright so I want to understand more
about the current pope, can you tell us what

00:22:23.549 --> 00:22:28.110
he has prioritized so far, and whether he’s
been successful?

00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:32.520
BYRNES: Whether he was successful or not remains
to be seen, but I think he has certainly made

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:35.539
an effort and has been given an enormous amount
of attention.

00:22:35.539 --> 00:22:38.600
He issued an encyclical on climate change
called Laudato Si.

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:42.520
A lot of people have talked about climate
change, but almost no one has talked about

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:47.159
it in the terms he talked about it, which
was that climate change is the greatest threat

00:22:47.159 --> 00:22:52.490
to the people around the world who have played
the least role in creating it.

00:22:52.490 --> 00:22:57.250
So we, in the United States and the West and
industrialized countries, have created this

00:22:57.250 --> 00:23:00.220
historic calamity known as climate change.

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:06.580
And yet, the people who are going to be most
immediately affected by the climate crisis,

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:11.779
dramatic weather events, tsunamis, hurricanes,
etc., and in some parts of the world actually

00:23:11.779 --> 00:23:17.070
losing their farming land and maybe even losing
their country, there are places in Southeast

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:18.179
Asia, which will actually be underwater.

00:23:18.179 --> 00:23:23.549
And the Pope said in his encyclical that not
only is climate change real and a challenge

00:23:23.549 --> 00:23:28.630
to the church's stewardship of God's creation,
which is how he talks about it, but it's also

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:34.450
a moral issue because you and I, when we get
on planes and cars and heat our houses and

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:40.500
have air conditioning, we're actually doing
damage in a kind of unethical way to the most

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:44.420
marginalized people on Earth who had no role
to play in this crisis.

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:50.309
SIERRA: Laudato Si focused catholic attention
on the climate crisis just as the Paris Climate

00:23:50.309 --> 00:23:51.480
accords were being written.

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:56.890
And some believe this changed the conversation
within catholic communities.

00:23:56.890 --> 00:24:02.330
SIERRA: Is there a flip side to the soft power
coin?

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:05.770
BYRNES: Well, it depends on one's view on
issues.

00:24:05.770 --> 00:24:09.160
Right, so you see this in American Catholicism
a lot.

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:12.750
And, and when the Pope is saying something
that a Catholic agrees with, they're like,

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:13.750
"Oh, that's my Pope.

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:14.750
Isn't that wonderful?

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:16.771
And then the Pope says something they don't
like and they say, "Well, that's just the

00:24:16.771 --> 00:24:17.771
Pope."

00:24:17.771 --> 00:24:18.771
SIERRA: Right.

00:24:18.771 --> 00:24:23.080
BYRNES: If someone is going to go out into
the public square, whether it's in the US,

00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:27.850
South Africa, China, wherever and say, "Well,
the Pope not only says that climate change

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:33.240
is real and that we have a religious responsibility
to protect the stewardship of God's creation,

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:37.169
but we have to do it because it's a moral
imperative for the poor and the marginalized."

00:24:37.169 --> 00:24:42.880
You say that, then what do you say about the
Pope's argument that gender ideology is a

00:24:42.880 --> 00:24:48.149
threat to human dignity and that marriage
can only be between one man and one woman

00:24:48.149 --> 00:24:53.779
or that abortion or the destruction of a fertilized
egg is always wrong in every sense, you know

00:24:53.779 --> 00:24:55.360
the Pope is not a liberal.

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:56.680
He's the Pope.

00:24:56.680 --> 00:25:01.810
SIERRA: It's worth jumping in to note that
these interviews were conducted before news

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:05.250
broke of the Supreme Court draft opinion on
abortion.

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:10.809
SIERRA: I mean speaking of those things, from
what I’ve heard about this pope, it seems

00:25:10.809 --> 00:25:14.299
like he has de-emphasized some of those issues.

00:25:14.299 --> 00:25:20.390
I mean has that changed the Catholic conversation
about LGBTQ issues, or abortion at all?

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:25.539
BYRNES: He hasn't changed any policies or
teachings, but he has certainly tried to change

00:25:25.539 --> 00:25:26.880
the conversation.

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:31.190
He came to the United States some years ago,
I forget the year, and he spoke before congress,

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:34.960
a joint session of Congress, a very, very
rare honor given to anyone, much less than

00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:36.590
a Catholic Pope.

00:25:36.590 --> 00:25:39.289
He started to talk about threats to human
life and human dignity.

00:25:39.289 --> 00:25:42.779
I thought, okay, here comes the abortion paragraph.

00:25:42.779 --> 00:25:43.779
But it wasn't.

00:25:43.779 --> 00:25:45.580
It was the capital punishment paragraph.

00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:47.400
He never mentioned abortion.

00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:55.370
Now, Pope Francis is strenuously, unambiguously,
clearly opposed to liberalized abortion law.

00:25:55.370 --> 00:25:57.899
But that was interesting that he did that.

00:25:57.899 --> 00:26:03.429
He also said in a very complicated conversation
some years ago having to do with gay priests,

00:26:03.429 --> 00:26:06.500
"Well, who am I to judge?"

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:09.590
Now, many people said, "You're the Pope.

00:26:09.590 --> 00:26:12.010
Isn't that what you do?"

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:18.169
And he has spent a lot of time in the ensuing
years trying to be clear about what he meant

00:26:18.169 --> 00:26:19.169
by that.

00:26:19.169 --> 00:26:22.100
And, I think what he meant was that if you
were a Catholic priest, you were celibate.

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:27.679
You were not engaged in sexual relations,
but you had same sex identity and were a celibate

00:26:27.679 --> 00:26:30.110
gay man, who am I to judge?

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:31.340
It's not my business.

00:26:31.340 --> 00:26:37.179
But just saying publicly, who am I to judge,
in the context of homosexuality did kind of

00:26:37.179 --> 00:26:41.090
open up a little bit of space for this conversation
to be a little different.

00:26:41.090 --> 00:26:45.360
But I really don't want to be seen as overstating
that space.

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:48.720
The Pope remains strenuously opposed to same
sex marriage.

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:53.280
And, of course, the church continues throughout
the world and certainly in the United States

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:55.610
to be a major spokesman against reproductive
freedom.

00:26:55.610 --> 00:26:57.500
So those policies haven't changed.

00:26:57.500 --> 00:27:00.230
But I like the way you said, "Did he change
the conversation?"

00:27:00.230 --> 00:27:01.230
A little bit.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:06.380
That's one of the reasons why some conservatives
are so unhappy with him.

00:27:06.380 --> 00:27:07.380
SIERRA: Cool.

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:11.960
You know not changing policy but, I guess,
changing what he thinks is worth focusing

00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:13.000
on as well.

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:15.220
BYRNES: I mean maybe even less than that.

00:27:15.220 --> 00:27:16.250
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:17.250
Okay.

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:24.450
BYRNES: His central emphasis is on the church
being a church of the poor, for the poor,

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:26.429
in order to help the marginalized.

00:27:26.429 --> 00:27:32.290
And, I think it is certainly true that in
that context, he would like a little bit less

00:27:32.290 --> 00:27:35.409
discussion of what we Americans call culture
war.

00:27:35.409 --> 00:27:37.740
It's not that he doesn't hold those positions.

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:40.510
He doesn't want to talk about that, I think
it's fair to say.

00:27:40.510 --> 00:27:45.529
He wants to talk about refugees, climate,
and poverty.

00:27:45.529 --> 00:27:50.820
And therefore, he's tried to say, "Let's talk
a little bit less about that and a little

00:27:50.820 --> 00:27:51.820
bit more about this."

00:27:51.820 --> 00:27:55.679
SIERRA: The Catholic church tends not to change.

00:27:55.679 --> 00:28:00.370
But, the Pope has enormous power to shape
what it focuses on.

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:03.210
And that’s why who he is matters.

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:08.000
Whether you are catholic or not, the choices
he makes could impact your life and the things

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:10.260
you care about in the world.

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:15.200
One person who can testify to this is our
guest, Ambassador Campbell, who grew up in

00:28:15.200 --> 00:28:21.640
Northern Ireland during the troubles, a period
of prolonged and deadly conflict between Prostestant

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:24.210
UK loyalists and Irish Catholics.

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:27.180
CAMPBELL: I remember as a nine-year-old, in
1979.

00:28:27.180 --> 00:28:30.429
It was the first visit of a pope to Ireland.

00:28:30.429 --> 00:28:35.240
And the pope couldn't come to Northern Ireland
so we all had to travel across the border

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:37.620
as close as we could get.

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:44.110
And he went to a place in Drogheda, which
had been a scene of one of Cromwell's massacres.

00:28:44.110 --> 00:28:50.370
And I remember in 1979, the troubles were
at their height, and it had come just a few

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:52.929
weeks after very horrific events.

00:28:52.929 --> 00:28:57.750
In bombings, and including the murder of Lord
Mountbatten.

00:28:57.750 --> 00:28:59.120
Very tense time.

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:04.419
And here is this pope who we all know had
come from the experience of communism in Central

00:29:04.419 --> 00:29:05.920
and Eastern Europe.

00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:09.570
But calling on the men of violence to lay
down their arms.

00:29:09.570 --> 00:29:12.100
And it wasn't safe language.

00:29:12.100 --> 00:29:15.559
It was bold, prophetic language.

00:29:15.559 --> 00:29:18.980
And it also understood the nature of the problem.

00:29:18.980 --> 00:29:21.740
It wasn't black and white.

00:29:21.740 --> 00:29:28.750
He also talked about the need for dignity,
and for the state to uphold the dignity of

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:32.490
each human being, irrespective of their difference.

00:29:32.490 --> 00:29:38.950
And I think, for me, I never forgot that,
that firstly here was someone that came, that

00:29:38.950 --> 00:29:44.529
drew attention to this issue, that wasn't
afraid to challenge power.

00:29:44.529 --> 00:29:48.780
The power of the British government to do
the right thing in relation to its administration

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:51.880
of Northern Ireland at that time when it wasn't
right.

00:29:51.880 --> 00:29:59.150
It wasn't correct, but equally, not to flinch
from a message to people inside the Catholic

00:29:59.150 --> 00:30:03.100
community who had chosen to take up arms.

00:30:03.100 --> 00:30:06.200
And he would've seen that as his primary goal.

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:07.960
That's who I must communicate to.

00:30:07.960 --> 00:30:14.399
And I must ask you-and I think that for me,
I'm not sure that another figure could have

00:30:14.399 --> 00:30:20.220
delivered that message, at that time, in that
way, unless they had come out of, say, an

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:24.580
experience like a Pole living under communism.

00:30:24.580 --> 00:30:30.990
But that was a very powerful, prophetic voice
that I think resonated.

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:35.060
And certainly sort of would've encouraged
people to go down the route of peace.

00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:39.090
And eventually in that case, the peace process.

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:45.490
And it risks taking a position that may be
criticized in the here and now, but will stand

00:30:45.490 --> 00:30:51.830
the test of time diplomatically and politically
going forward.

00:30:51.830 --> 00:30:58.659
SIERRA: Since this is our last episode with
our fabulous intern Roshni, we are going to

00:30:58.659 --> 00:31:00.529
have her read us out today.

